\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Wall-clock time vs.\ particle count $N$ on a log--log scale. All implementations exhibit $\mathcal {O}(N^2)$ scaling; parallel versions separate from sequential above $N \approx 400$.}}{20}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces OpenMP speedup vs.\ $N$ for 2, 4, and 8 threads. Speedup improves with $N$ as computation outweighs reduction merge overhead.}}{20}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces MPI speedup vs.\ $N$ for 2 and 4 processes. The near-flat curves confirm that communication overhead does not amortise with increasing $N$.}}{21}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Hybrid speedup vs.\ $N$. Fewer MPI processes with more OpenMP threads ($2P{\times }4T$) consistently outperforms the reverse configuration ($4P{\times }2T$).}}{21}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Parallel efficiency vs.\ $N$ for 8-worker configurations. OpenMP sustains the highest efficiency; Hybrid improves with $N$ but plateaus below OpenMP.}}{22}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Wall-clock time at $N=100$ demonstrating the overhead-dominated regime. Most parallel configurations are slower than sequential because $\mathcal {O}(N^2)$ computation (4{,}950 pairs) takes less time than parallel startup costs; MPI 4P is only a marginal exception in this benchmark run.}}{22}{figure.caption.10}%
